Iris — Licensing
================

Iris consists of a core type-bridge library, an OS utility layer, and
a public SDK. The core is GPL-2.0 (with an SDK linking exception, see
below); the OS utility layer is Apache-2.0; the SDK is MIT.

Copyright (c) 2026 Iris Contributors.


Components
----------

  Core  (src/, include/ — except os.hpp)   GNU General Public License v2.0
                                            with the Iris SDK Linking Exception
                                            → https://www.gnu.org/licenses/old-licenses/gpl-2.0.html

  OS utilities  (src/os/, include/os.hpp)   Apache License, Version 2.0
                                            → https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0

  SDK  (sdk/)                                MIT License
                                            → https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT


Iris SDK Linking Exception
--------------------------

As a special exception, the copyright holders grant you permission to
link your program against unmodified builds of libiris (and its OS
utility satellite libirisos) exclusively through the MIT-licensed SDK
headers located under `sdk/`, and to distribute the resulting combined
work under any license of your choice, provided that:

  (a) your program uses only symbols and interfaces declared in the SDK
      headers — that is, it does not link against, `#include`, use, or
      otherwise depend on any header, symbol, or interface located under
      `include/` or `src/` outside of the `sdk/` tree;

  (b) libiris and libirisos themselves are linked in unmodified form.
      Any local modification to the sources or headers of libiris or
      libirisos, or any static-linking of their object code into your
      program, causes this exception to no longer apply. The core
      remains available under the plain GPL-2.0 terms in that case;

  (c) you preserve the copyright notice, this exception, and the
      GPL-2.0 licence text when redistributing libiris or libirisos.

The intent is that any C-ABI-speaking language — C, C++, Rust, Go,
Python via ctypes, Java via JNA, and so on — can `dlopen` or
dynamically link against libiris through the SDK boundary without
inheriting copyleft, in the same spirit as the OpenJDK Classpath
Exception and the libgcc Runtime Library Exception. Modifications to
the core remain GPL-2.0 and must flow back.

This exception does NOT apply if you modify libiris or libirisos, if
you statically link them, or if your program depends on internal
(non-SDK) headers. In any of those cases, the plain terms of GPL-2.0
apply to the combined work.

If you have questions about whether a specific use is covered, the
principle is: **use through the sdk/ headers only → exempt; touch
anything under src/ or include/ → GPL-2.0 applies as normal**.


Iris Apache-2.0 Build Dependency Exception
------------------------------------------

As a special exception, the copyright holders grant you permission to
compile and link this library with Apache-2.0 licensed libraries that
are used solely as optional build-time dependencies — included only
through their declared public headers, enabled by a designated CMake
option, and not contributing to a separate runtime-loadable module —
and to distribute the resulting combined work, provided that:

  (a) the Apache-2.0 dependency is enabled only through the designated
      CMake option (e.g. -DIRIS_STDEXEC=ON) and is not unconditionally
      embedded into the core;

  (b) any modifications to the iris core (sources under src/ and headers
      under include/, excluding the Apache-2.0 OS utility layer) remain
      governed by the GNU General Public License v2.0;

  (c) you comply with the terms of the Apache-2.0 license for the
      Apache-2.0 licensed components, including preserving copyright
      notices and the NOTICE file where required.

The stdexec library (github.com/NVIDIA/stdexec, Apache-2.0) is a named
example of a build dependency covered by this exception.

This exception does NOT apply to derived works of the iris core itself.
A modified core must be redistributed under GPL-2.0; the exception
releases only the build-time inclusion of Apache-2.0 headers from
copyleft propagation.


Summary
-------

You may use, modify, and redistribute the core under the terms of the
GNU General Public License v2.0. Any derived work that incorporates
the core must be distributed under the same license.

The SDK linking exception releases users who link only through the
sdk/ C ABI from copyleft propagation — any C, C++, Rust, Go, Python,
Java, or other C-ABI-speaking program can `dlopen` or dynamically link
against an unmodified libiris built from this source tree without
inheriting GPL, provided the program does not reach into internal
headers or statically link the core.

The OS utility layer (ls, ps, env commands and their type definitions)
is released under the Apache License 2.0 and may be incorporated into
proprietary or differently-licensed projects without restriction,
provided the Apache 2.0 attribution requirements are met.

The SDK (sdk/*.h, sdk/cpp/, sdk/py/, and future language bindings)
is released under the MIT License and may be freely embedded in any
codebase without restriction beyond MIT's short attribution clause.

SPDX identifiers on each file record the applicable licence per the
component boundaries above.
